# openOODA/SCRIPTING.oot — `.oo` script standards

**Audience:** anyone writing a `.oo` script under `ooda/scripts/`.
**Goal:** every script reads as a story, not a receipt.

## 1. File structure

Every `.oo` script has five parts, in order:

1. Title block (5 lines max)
2. Imports (minimal)
3. Constants / fixtures
4. `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap)` — the story as code
5. (Optional) private helpers

## 2. Title block

<code>

// # <Title>
//
// Logline: <one sentence, present tense, active voice>.
//
// Setup: <preconditions>.
//
// Beats:
//   1. <beat 1>
//   2. <beat 2>
//   3. <beat 3>

</code>

## 3. Vocabulary

Use these verbs in `main`:

- `scenario(name)` — open a named story.
- `when` — begin the action.
- `and` — chain the next beat.
- `then` — the resolution.
- `given` — setup precondition.
- `expect_*` — assertions (see `ooda/std/scenario.oo`).
  - `expect_present`, `expect_absent`
  - `expect_line_count`, `expect_substring`, `expect_count`
  - `expect_quiet`, `expect_clean`
  - `expect_no_phantoms`, `expect_equal`, `expect_lt`
- `check` — a one-shot test.

## 4. Naming

- File names: snake_case (e.g., `harness_f26_residual_smoke.oo`).
- Helper functions: snake_case verbs (e.g., `sh_clean`, `expect_line_count`).
- Variables: named after the story element, not `x` or `tmp`.

## 5. Anti-patterns

DO NOT:

- Write `if r.is_err() { bad(); fail = 1; }` in `main`. Use `expect_*` helpers.
- Use `process_exec` directly when `sh_*` exists.
- Write a 200-line script. Split into multiple files.
- Use abstract words ("data", "value", "result"). Use specific ones.
- Print "Test 1:", "Test 2:". Use scenario names.
- Write a `.sh` script. Use `.oo`.

## 6. Cap requirements

`main` signature:

- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap)` — only process exec.
- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap)` — reads files.
- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap, env: &EnvCap)` — reads env.

Cap is injected by the runtime. Don't mint caps in scripts.

## 7. Verification

Run `ooda/scripts/script_lint.oo` on every `.oo` script before commit.
The linter checks:

- Title block present.
- Logline present.
- Beats present.
- No `process_exec` direct calls outside `main`.
- Line count ≤ 256.

